00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:18.970
Out of the myriad problems that emerged in
the world in Spring 2020, there is one that

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:24.680
is quietly being solved—one that has to
do with the behind-the-scenes of a globalized,

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.560
modern-day economy.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.670
Understanding it is easy.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.180
At its core, it’s a simple supply and demand
mismatch.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:39.280
You see, back in March 2020, when COVID-19
reached pandemic status, almost every airline

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.710
in the world drastically scaled back its flying.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.190
It varied from region to region and airline
to airline, but the common trend everywhere

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.420
was that, if any route got cut, it was almost
certainly a long-haul one.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:58.190
In the era of quarantine periods, border closings,
and other travel restrictions, there is near-zero

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:04.100
demand for long-haul, international travel,
so intercontinental flights were quickly removed

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.900
from airline schedules.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:12.070
For example, in April 2019, about 500 roundtrip
flights per day would fly between Europe and

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:18.210
the US, however, in April 2020, that number
was reduced to about 80 per day.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.380
That represents between an 80% and 90% reduction.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.960
Ignoring airline revenue, you’d think that
that would be a loss-free situation, though:

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.320
it reduces carbon emissions, many governments
are covering the salaries of furloughed workers,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.090
it reduces airline expenditure, and nobody’s
riding in the cabins anyways, but that doesn’t

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.600
consider what rides below the cabin.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.560
On nearly every intercontinental flight, in
the belly hold, among the hundreds of passenger

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.580
bags, there is cargo.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:51.229
In fact, 45% of the world’s air cargo is
carried in this method.

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:56.140
Now, the math to explain the supply and demand
mismatch is simple.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.880
The proportion of cargo carried on passenger
aircraft varies from route to route, so 60%

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.210
of Western Europe to US air cargo, for example,
flies on passenger planes.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:10.849
80% to 90% of transatlantic passenger flights
are not flying, which means that about 50%

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:15.569
of air cargo capacity in this market has disappeared
in a matter of weeks.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:21.620
The situation is less severe in other markets,
but, industry-wide, the overall air cargo

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.260
capacity reduction was about 23% in March,
2020.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.870
So that’s the supply figure, but what about
demand?

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.099
As a huge proportion of the world entered
various lockdown scenarios, economic forecasts

00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:37.139
plummeted.

00:02:37.139 --> 00:02:41.419
The extent of the financial toll will not
be clear for a while, but it is clear that

00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:43.499
it is deeply negative.

00:02:43.499 --> 00:02:46.389
This all has an effect on air cargo demand.

00:02:46.389 --> 00:02:51.389
On a macro level, consumers are buying less,
which means less is shipped, which means companies

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:55.959
are buying less, which means less is shipped,
which means suppliers are making less, which

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:57.230
means less is shipped.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.989
On a micro level, different jurisdictions
worldwide differ on the exact details of their

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:06.841
Coronavirus response, but in many places,
factories are shut down which means supply

00:03:06.841 --> 00:03:08.540
chains are cut.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.900
So, from an economic perspective, one would
predict that demand would almost certainly

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:20.529
be down, but like all things in the age of
Coronavirus, there is another dominant perspective—that

00:03:20.529 --> 00:03:22.560
of public health.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.829
From almost the moment COVID-19 emerged, it
became clear that it was a highly infectious

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:32.629
virus, which means that a crucial component
of the safe treatment of an infected individual

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:37.309
is the use of personal protective equipment,
or PPE.

00:03:37.309 --> 00:03:42.370
In this context, that means, masks, shields,
gowns, and more, which healthcare facilities

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:47.510
always stock, but never in the quantity needed
to cover nearly universal staff usage for

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.930
a long period.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:53.919
That means that, in order to reduce the risk
of staff exposure, nearly every hospital in

00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:56.620
the world needed more PPE.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:02.150
Purely by chance, it turns out that the number
one medical PPE producing country in the world

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:06.329
was also the first country to experience COVID-19—China.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:11.040
Before the pandemic, the country produced
20 million medical masks per day, or about

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.159
half of the world’s supply.

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:18.010
By the beginning of March, though, this had
already been scaled up to more than 100 million

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:19.010
per day.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:23.710
It was at exactly this time when, simultaneously,
China’s manufacturing industry started to

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:28.390
inch back to life and the virus truly took
hold of the rest of the world.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:34.110
With how fast COVID-19 ramped up, many hospitals
in hotspots like Italy, Spain, and the US

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.720
were reusing masks and, even with that technique,
were down to only days of supply, so they

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.620
needed supply from China, and other manufacturing
hubs, as fast as possible.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.880
In the logistics world, “as fast as possible,”
means air cargo.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:54.930
Thanks to this unprecedented and urgent demand
for PPE and other medical supplies, demand

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:59.370
for air cargo only dropped by 15% in March,
2020.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:05.390
Considering air cargo capacity dropped by
23%, that means that there was an 8% gap between

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:06.780
supply and demand.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.650
In the context of this enormous industry,
8% is huge, and so the laws of supply and

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.140
demand followed their course.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:20.400
The cost to transport goods by air spiked,
especially in the crucial Asia to US and Europe

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.540
markets.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.190
In the last two weeks of March, air cargo
rates from Hong Kong to North America jumped

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:31.780
27% above normal, while rates from Shanghai
to Europe increased by 50%.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:37.390
Of course, after a supply and demand mismatch
economic forces push companies to increase

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.680
supply, which is exactly what happened.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.240
Cargo airlines that could massively ramped
up their flights.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.110
Kalitta Air, for example, an American cargo
airline, brought some aircraft out of storage

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.440
and increased its long-haul flying by nearly
50%.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.440
However, there is only so much that these
cargo airlines can do.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:02.130
They only have so many aircraft, so many pilots,
and so many mechanics so they can’t massively

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.550
ramp up their operations in a short time.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:09.810
Who did have extra capacity, though, were
passenger airlines, and that’s where a small

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.680
solution emerged.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.220
A plane with no passengers is not necessarily
an empty plane.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:20.860
An average airline earns 15% to 20% of its
revenue through cargo, but keep in mind what

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.930
an average airline looks like.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.900
Most of the average North American airlines’
flying is domestic and short-haul, where little

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.540
cargo is carried, so the proportion of revenue
coming from cargo on a long-haul flight is

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.220
far higher.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.800
Consider too that, on a normal long-haul passenger
flight, a considerable portion of the cargo

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.430
hold is filled with baggage so that 15 to
20% is derived from only some of the capacity

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.070
on only some flights.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:51.860
Airlines soon realized that there was a possibility
of turning a profit flying a passenger plane

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.010
with no passengers.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:07:00.240
Now, normally, a passenger a330, for example,
has capacity for 38 thousand pounds or 17

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.200
thousand kilograms of cargo in its belly hold.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.490
That’s compared to the freighter version
of the a330 which can carry 132 thousand pounds

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:13.170
or 60 thousand kilograms of freight thanks
to its completely empty cabin.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:19.720
That means that the passenger a330 can only
carry 29% as much cargo as its freighter counterpart.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:25.070
However, considering that freight rates have
risen by as much as 50%, that means that flying

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:29.430
these aircraft can earn about 44% as much
per flight as their freighter counterparts

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.130
normally do.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:35.930
Making less than half as much as cargo airlines
clearly would not make flying passenger aircraft

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:39.930
for cargo purposes worth it, but there is
more to consider.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:46.040
First, jet fuel prices are at an all-time
low, largely in response to all-time low demand.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:52.169
The same gallon that would have cost airlines
$2.20 in January, 2020 costs about 40 cents

00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:56.419
in late-April, 2020, and those prices are
continuing to drop.

00:07:56.419 --> 00:08:01.440
In normal conditions, fuel is airlines’
number one cost so having that drop by a factor

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.500
of five reduces the cost to operate a flight
dramatically.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:09.920
That means that they need to earn far less
than what a cargo airline does in normal conditions

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:10.920
to break-even.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:14.960
In addition, most airlines are paying their
pilots and other staff anyways.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.070
In the US, for example, airlines are required,
as a condition of their government relief

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:24.010
funding, to not lay off or furlough any staff,
so the marginal staffing cost to operate an

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:26.050
additional flight is minimal.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:31.530
Thirdly, with no passengers, airlines have
quickly innovated new ways to carry cargo.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.370
Many airlines have loaded their passenger
cabins with cargo placed directly on top of

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.129
and fastened to seats.

00:08:37.129 --> 00:08:42.289
This dramatically increases complexity and
loading time, but it also dramatically increases

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:43.550
capacity.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.519
All of these conditions and circumstances
combine to the point that, for this brief

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:52.880
moment in time, economics dictate that an
airline can turn a profit flying a passenger

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.410
plane without passengers.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:09:00.790
Big airlines like American Airlines, Air Canada,
Lufthansa, and Air New Zealand began cargo-only

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.800
flights—so many, in fact, that United airlines,
for example, had up to dozens of passenger-less

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.779
passenger planes airborne at the same time
all around the world.

00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:14.850
This also created flights that have never
happened before, like a 16-hour nonstop cargo-only

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.940
trip from Sydney to Toronto by Air Canada.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.250
But it wasn’t all just big airlines on long
flights.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:24.120
Smaller airlines too got in on the game.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.740
Air Greenland started a link on a tiny propeller
Dash-8 to transport Coronavirus tests for

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.089
processing in a Danish lab, Titan airways
flew medical supplies to the remote island

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:38.959
of St Helena on a passenger a318, and Wizz
Air flew their passenger a321’s to Shanghai

00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:42.490
via Kazakstan to transport medical supplies
to Hungary.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:48.249
These circumstances have also led to a dramatic
reshuffling of rank and order in the aviation

00:09:48.249 --> 00:09:49.249
world.

00:09:49.249 --> 00:09:53.130
The list of the world’s busiest airports,
in terms of annual aircraft movements, is

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:57.920
normally headed by Atlanta, followed by Chicago,
Los Angeles, Dallas, Beijing, and a number

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:59.720
of other major hub airports.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.559
Normally, you wouldn’t expect an airport
from America’s third least populous state

00:10:04.559 --> 00:10:10.519
anywhere on the first pages of that list,
however, for a brief moment in April, 2020,

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:12.009
that was the case.

00:10:12.009 --> 00:10:18.240
Anchorage airport, in Alaska, became the world’s
busiest airport thanks to its strategic location.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.069
It has long been a busy cargo airport as it
acts as stopover point for cargo flights from

00:10:23.069 --> 00:10:24.450
Asia to North America.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.579
They stop here because it is more efficient
for aircraft, on long-haul flights, to take

00:10:28.579 --> 00:10:33.639
less fuel and more cargo and refuel halfway
through than fly nonstop to their destination.

00:10:33.639 --> 00:10:38.869
Also, in some cases, freight is sorted and
exchanged between aircraft in Anchorage.

00:10:38.869 --> 00:10:44.540
In April 2020, the US ranked number one in
the world for new and total Coronavirus cases,

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:50.029
so it had a tremendous need for medical supplies,
which are overwhelmingly produced in Asia,

00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:54.360
so hundreds of cargo aircraft per day passed
through Anchorage on their way between Asia

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.779
and the US.

00:10:55.779 --> 00:11:01.309
So, while every other major airport in the
US fell into relative silence, Anchorage was

00:11:01.309 --> 00:11:03.350
busier than ever.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:08.709
There is, however, one final issue that the
air cargo world is facing.

00:11:08.709 --> 00:11:14.790
Beyond the short-term issue of too much demand,
the long-term issue is that this demand will

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.910
not last.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:21.059
Global air cargo demand closely tracks with
global economic health.

00:11:21.059 --> 00:11:25.660
Every indicator suggests that the economy
will not emerge from the COVID-19 pandemic

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:30.759
even close to as strong as when it entered,
so there will undoubtably be some slow months

00:11:30.759 --> 00:11:32.829
and years for air cargo.

00:11:32.829 --> 00:11:37.160
Once urgent demand for medical supplies eases,
and buyers have the time to switch to slower

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:43.589
forms of shipping, the air cargo world will
almost certainly follow the fate of its passenger-carrying

00:11:43.589 --> 00:11:45.110
colleagues.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:50.279
On the exact same day as this video goes out,
we released a brand-new, hour-long documentary

00:11:50.279 --> 00:11:52.769
called, “The Final Years of Majuro.”

00:11:52.769 --> 00:11:58.309
It’s about the isolated nation of the Marshall
Islands and, specifically, what life is like

00:11:58.309 --> 00:12:03.369
when you know your nation is coming to an
end—when where you live has an expiration

00:12:03.369 --> 00:12:04.369
date.

